Abstract
The continuing increase of the efficiency of perovskite solar cells has pushed the internal quantum efficiency approaching $100\%$ , which means the light-to-carrier and then the following carrier transportation and extraction are no longer limiting factors in photoelectric conversion efficiency of perovskite solar cells. However, the optimal efficiency is still far lower than the Shockley–Queisser efficiency limit, especially for those inverted perovskite solar cells, indicating significant fraction of light do not transmit into the active perovskite layer to be absorbed there. Here a planar inverted perovskite solar cell (ITO/PTAA/perovskite/ $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}/$ bathocuproine (BCP)/Ag) is chosen as an example and we show that the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of it can be significantly improved by simply texturing the Poly [bis (4-phenyl)(2,4,6- trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) layer. By washing the film prepared from mixed polymer solution of PTAA and Polystyrene (PS), a textured PTAA/perovskite interface is introduced on the light-input side of perovskite to inhibit internal optical reflection. The reduction of optical loss by this simple texture method increases the EQE and then the photocurrent of ITO/PTAA/perovskite $\mathrm{{'PC}_{61}}$ BM/BCP/Ag device with the magnitude about $10\%$ . At the same time, this textured PTAA benefits the band edge absorption in this planar solar cell. The large increase of the short-circuit current together with the increase of fill factor pushes the efficiency of this inverted perovskite solar cell from $18.3\%$ up to an efficiency over $20.8\%$ . By using anti-reflection coating on glass to let more light into the device, the efficiency is further improved to $21.6\%$ , further demonstrating the importance of light management in perovskite solar cells.
Keywords: internal optical reflection, optical matching, PTAA, interface texture, perovskite solar cell
Organic-inorganic halide perovskite solar cells have revolutionized the community of photovoltaics, attracting more and more attention around the world.1, 2 It has climbed to a high photoelectric conversion efficiency (PCE) of more than $24.02\%^{3}$ since the invention of the first $3.8\%^{4}$ device. The proceeding rapid advances in device efficiency has finally reached its plateau after a decade of optimization.5 However, there is still a big gap between the achieved experimental PCE and theoretical Shockley-Queisser (SQ) limit based on its favorable bandgap $\mathrm{(}{\approx}1.55\ \mathrm{eV}$ , adjustable6-9) of perovskite. To further boost the PCE of perovskite solar cells, one way is to reduce bandgap by introducing larger CH- $\cdot(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}^{+}$ $(\mathrm{FA}^{+})$ cation10-12 to increase the S-Q limit. Indeed, perovskites incorporated with $\mathrm{FA^{+}}$ cation which have relatively smaller bandgaps did show higher currents and better efficiencies.13 The champion efficiency of perovskite solar cells has been achieved by using those smaller bandgap materials.5, 13-15 Another way is to further improve all parameters (including short-circuit current $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ , opencircuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ and fill factor (FF)) for certain perovskite material by more advanced techniques, such as additive16, 17 or solvent,18, 19 interface20-23 and materials24 or energy level25, 26 engineering. Due to numerous devotions on perovskite solar cells, both $\mathrm{V_{OC}}^{27}$ and $\mathrm{FF}^{28}$ have approached to their S-Q limit, leaving only $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ to be further promoted.29 For example, the $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ discrepancy is about $3\mathrm{\mA}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ between achieved value and the S-Q limit for the perovskite with $1.55\mathrm{eV}$ bandgap. When looking deeply into those best performance devices with EQE around $85\%$ , the internal quantum efficiency (IQE) is found to approach to the maximum of $100\%^{7,30,31}$ without exception, which indicates all absorbed photons have been converted into photon-generated carriers. Thus, this means that optical loss but not the electric properties has become one of the most important bottlenecks for further improving efficiency of perovskite solar cells.32 To achieve the minimum optical loss, the light reflection from the device and the light absorption in the active perovskite film are expected to be minimized and maximized respectively. Due to the high extinction efficiency of perovskite materials, the maximization of light absorption is relatively simple by just using thicker active layer33-35 and a highly refractive back electrode to double the optical path. Texture structure is an efficient way to minimize the optical loss. In fact, there were several studies about texturing the perovskite or electron transfer layer (ETL) in normal perovskite solar cells. For example, textured $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\mathrm{MA})\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ morphology formed through a thin mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ seeding layer and a gas-assisted crystallization method was reported by Zheng et al.36 The textured morphology reduced light transmitted from perovskite to HTL and then the metal electrode, significant improving the light harvest in longer wavelength range. Similarly, simulation of planar normal structure with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL made by Eerden et al.37 indicated that the roughness increase at the perovskite back side was beneficial to reduce the optical loss. Though texture structure was used at the interface between HTL and perovskite layer in inverted PSCs, it was fabricated by metal oxides and was used to improve charge transport but not to reduce the optical loss. Anti-reflection coating is an effective way to minimize optical loss due to reflection, but it can only reduce the reflection between air and the glass substrate. When organic materials are used as the transport layer, optical loss due to the internal reflection caused by low refractive index of organic materials becomes an obstacle to achieve high power conversion efficiency. Other methods including multi-nanolayer films38 used colorful cells, plasmonic structure39 used for enhancing absorption of active layer and metal−organic frameworks40 for improving transparency of transport layer have also been reported. The refractive index matching and then the device configuration plays the determined role of light reflection in a multilayer structure.37 The world record efficiency was based on mesoporous normal structure with perovskite having mixed cations3, 15 which benefits not only from the refractive index matching (no abrupt refractive index discrepancy from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to perovskite layer) but also from the beneficial light scattering due to mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Planar inverted perovskite solar cell is a simpler structure and less energy consumption for fabricating. Though inverted devices have been greatly improved in recent years, their efficiency is still lower than mesoporous normal structured perovskite solar cells.15 Deep analysis shows that the refractive index configuration is far from optimization in this planar inverted structure perovskite solar cell. For example, PTAA is an ideal hole extraction layer (HTL) for an inverted perovskite solar cell.15, 27 This polymer has the irreplaceable advantages over metal oxides, such as low temperature processing, simple preparation, and flexibility. A lot of works26, 41 have been done and obtained many great achievements based on PTAA. However, PTAA is not a good choice in inverted configuration in term of its low refractive index. When light is incident from a material to another material with large refractive index discrepancy, it will be reflected more than that when the refractive index difference is small. A rough estimation of more than $3.5\%$ of light will be reflected back into ITO substrate due to the low refractive index of PTAA compared with high refractive index of the active perovskite film. This internal optical loss due to reflection is much higher than the reflection from metal oxides such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in planar normal structure, which is only about $1\%$ . Thus, to keep the PTAA in the planar inverted structure to fully utilize its advantages, a way must be found out to solve this internal optical reflection from the PTAA/perovskite interface. Texture is widely used method to reduce reflection in photovoltaic community42 besides anti-reflection coating, which is not applicable for reducing this internal optical reflection because anti-reflection coating layer is normally an insulator. Theoretical calculations show that optical engineering including texture can increase light utilization by $20\text{\textperthousand}$ .43
However, to our best knowledge, there is no report about coordinated optical matching through direct texturing the front side HTL or perovskite in a planar inverted device. Paetzold et al.44 used a photolithographic technique to form the nano-array on ITO and tried to transfer this texture up to the front side HTL or even perovskite film in an inverted structure. Though this morphology transfer increased the light absorption in perovskite, it is not a direct way to texture the front side HTL or perovskite film. At the same time, this morphology transfer process is very complicated, and the cost is very high. The goal of optical management is to urge more light transmitted into perovskite film to push the EQE close to IQE which has already been nearly $100\%$ . Considering the excellent properties of PTAA and the simple fabrication process, the inverted structure is chosen to explore the potential of optical management in this study. And taking the lessons from optical management in normal structure and theoretical calculation, we believe that the introduction of texture directly into the front side HTL or perovskite can largely reduce the internal reflection due to the abrupt refractive index change from PTAA to perovskite film.
Herein, we report a method to reduce the internal reflection from the PTAA/perovskite interface by texturing PTAA through simply mixing PS with PTAA and then washing them away from the PTAA film. By using this simple but very effective texture method into the planar inverted perovskite solar cell with the structure of ITO/PTAA/MAPbI3(Cl)/PC61BM/BCP/Ag, a very high PCE of $21.6\%$ has been achieved with the help of anti-reflection coating technique. Compared with the reference devices without texturing PTAA, the $\mathrm{\DeltaJ_{SC}}$ is increased substantially with almost the same $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ and slightly increased FF. Experimental results confirmed that the reduction of internal optical reflection by texturing PTAA is the main reason for the increased $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ . More importantly, our study provides a facile cost-effective solution for optical management in planar inverted perovskite devices with low refractive index polymers as the HTL. Our work also demonstrates that optical management of photovoltaic devices is not only an essential part of commercialization, but also a key factor for device structure design and material selection.
## Results and Discussions
Optical Model and Theoretical Basis. We obtained the refractive index (n), extinction coefficient (k) values of each layer (including glass, ITO, PTAA, $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}(\mathrm{Cl}))$ using an ellipsometer, as shown in figure S1 in supporting information. Based on Fresnel equation described in equation (1), the reflectance of an inverted structure perovskite solar cell shown in Figure 1a with ideal flat surface under normal incidence condition can be calculated. For example, by using PTAA as the HTL in Figure 1a, the reflectance at 500, 600 and $750~\mathrm{nm}$ can be estimated as $3.3\%$ , $3.2\%$ and $3.8\%$ , respectively. Such a strong reflection is nearly equivalent to the reflection at glass/air interface, which has been reported that this strong reflection would lose 4.2 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of photocurrent.37 The optical configuration of a typical $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based normal structure device and a PTAA based inverted structure device are schematically compared in Figure 1a and 1b, which is consistent with the previous reports.45 The size of the arrow represents the light intensity. The main reflection loss in Figure 1a is from both the air/glass and the HTL/perovskite (internal) interfaces while in Figure 1b is mainly from air/glass interface. Figure 1c schematically demonstrates the introduction of texture at the PTAA/perovskite interface to effectively reduce the internal light reflection compared with Figure 1a. The role of texturing the HTL/perovskite (internal) interface together with the benefit of anti-reflection are demonstrated in Figure 1d by simulating the device absorption with different textured interfaces.
Figure 1. Solar cell architectures. Schematically ideal light transmissions and reflections of a) the typical normal structure b) the typical inverted structure solar cells based on Fresnel equation. c) Schematic optical transmission and reflection of our textured device, d) the absorption of the devices calculated by simulation.
