R=\left({\frac{n_{1}-n_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}}\right)^{2}
$$
where $\mathbf{n}_{1},\mathbf{n}_{2}$ represent the refractive index of the materials on each side of the interface, and the R represents the reflectivity of normal incidence at the interface.
Here, the texture is treated as a roughness of a certain film, which is a parameter used in Bruggeman Effective Medium Approximation, an open source program based on the Python programming language and is available on the website (numpy.scipy.org).
The absorption of the device with larger roughness of PTAA $(50\ \mathrm{nm})$ is effectively improved in the whole spectrum compared with plane multilayer. The absorption can be further improved with the anti-reflection coating at the air/glass interface, as shown in Figure 1d and confirmed by the experimental results given later. Another advantage of increased band edge absorption by using texture PTAA can be found from this simple simulation, which is can be ascribed to the scattering at the PTAA/perovskite interface. So, interface texture should be an effective mean to increase photocurrent of inverted perovskite solar cells with textured PTAA HTL.
Preparation and Characteristics of Texture Interface. Borrowing from the method of demixing a binary polymer blend during spin coating,45 the device with the expected texture interface was prepared as schematically shown in Figure 2a-d. First, a binary mixed polymer solution was obtained by mixing PS and PTAA in the same chlorobenzene solvent and then spin-coated it onto cleaned ITO to prepare a mixed PTAA(PS) HTL layer, as shown in Figure 2b. After exposing the ITO with the mixed PTAA HTL layer to cyclohexane solvent, which can selectively dissolve PS, the substrate was rotated with a high-speed to get rid of the residual cyclohexane. During this rotation process, $0.2~\mathrm{ml}$ pure cyclohexane solvent was added dropwise to wash away the residual PS, and the texture morphology of PTAA was obtained after this treatment, as shown in Figure 2c. Next, a perovskite layer was prepared by one-step spin coating onto this textured PTAA. Due to the full coverage of perovskite precursor solution on the textured PTAA, the morphology of PTAA is successfully transferred to the bottom side of the perovskite layer, as shown in Figure 2d. Figure 2e shows the XPS results of pure PTAA, pure PS, mixed PTAA (PS) and texture PTAA. There is no N peak at $398.9\mathrm{eV}$ for pure PS film due to the absence of N element in PS. The C peak is located at $284\mathrm{eV}$ . The small N peak and then the very large ratio of C to N in mixed PTAA (PS) film confirms the large portion of PS in the mixed film. However, the Cto-N ratios of the texture PTAA and pure PTAA are almost the same, indicating the negligible residual of PS in the texture PTAA. Figure 2f-h, show the cross-section SEM images of a PTAA(PS) mixed film, a textured PTAA film and a textured PTAA/perovskite interface respectively. The three cross-section SEM images correspond to the fabrication steps shown in Figure 2b-d and the texture preparation proposed here is highly reproducible.
To understand the influence of PS for the final roughness of texture PTAA layer, different concentrations of PS were added into PTAA solution (PTAA to PS 5:12, 5:20 and 5:45). The SEM images of final textured PTAA are compared. The top view images of figure S2 in supporting information show that the textured PTAA is spherically scattered on the ITO surface. The cross-sectional images (figure S3 in supporting information) show that the film undulation is significantly increased with the increasing thickness of the PTAA film with uniformly distributed texture. By changing the ratio of PTAA to PS, the fluctuation of the textured film can be increased from 40 to $120\mathrm{nm}$ . At the same time, the texture PTAA presents a sleek hemisphere composed of pure PTAA, as described in the former XPS analysis and in Figure 2g. The enlarged cross section SEM images of perovskite film on plane PTAA and texture PTAA, which are also the semi-finished devices, are compared in Figures 3a and 3b. The texture structure can be clearly discerned in Figure 3b. The transmittance of glass/ITO/PTAA structures with plane and texture PTAA HTL layer is shown in Figures 3c. A strong reduction occurs near $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the plane PTAA structure and this reduction is more obvious for the texture PTAA structure. However, after $420~\mathrm{nm}$ , the introduction of texture does increase the transmittance of this glass/ITO/PTAA structure with texture PTAA HTL layer. The reduction at about $400\mathrm{nm}$ is due to the absorption of PTAA, as shown in the extinction coefficient curve in figure S1 in supporting information. The more reduction at around $400\mathrm{nm}$ for texture PTAA is due to the thicker texture PTAA and its larger optical path caused by its undulation surface, as shown in figures S3 and S4. The increase of transmittance by using texture PTAA HTL layer, which means more light can be transmitted into active perovskite layer, can be easily explained by the
PTAA/air refractive index mutation due to the presence of texture according to Fresnel's theorem. The transmittance of the glass/ITO/PTAA/Perovskite semi-finished device with plane and texture PTAA is shows in figure S5 in supporting information. Due to the scattering effect from the rough PTAA/perovskite interface, the optical path is extended and then less light transmit through the semi-finished device with texture PTAA even though more photons are available for perovskite layer. A sufficiently thick and highly refractive Ag top electrode was deposited to complete the device fabrication. Silver electrode can reflect most of the transmitted light back into perovskite film for the twice absorption. By using an integrating sphere, the diffuse reflectance spectrum from the glass side of complete devices was measured and given in Figure 3d. The results show that the textured PTAA/perovskite interface does greatly reduce the light reflection of the device. Thus, the transmittance measurement shown in Figure 3c of glass/ITO/PTAA indicates the more light transmitted through textured PTAA and then the transmittance measurement shown in figure S5 in supporting information of glass/ITO/PTAA/perovskite semi-finished devices indicates more captured photons in perovskite layer, which is further confirmed by the diffuse reflectance results of real devices shown in Figure 3d. This is exactly the dream of light management in solar cells: let as much as more photons transmitted into and be absorbed by the active layer. To further realize this dream, $125\mathrm{nm}$ magnesium fluoride $(\mathrm{MgF}_{2})$ anti-reflection layer was sputtered onto the utmost outside of glass surface to reduce the reflection loss from air/glass interface. By adding this anti-reflection layer, the reflectivity of the real device is further decreased, as shown in Figure 3d. The positive effect of this anti-reflection coating was also confirmed by transmittance comparison after $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ coating, as shown in figure S6 in supporting information.
Figure 3. The cross-section SEM image of the texture interface was obtained by washing away PS by cyclohexane solvent. a) pure-PTAA/perovskite interface b) texture-PTAA/perovskite interface. c) The transmittance of glass/ITO/PTAA with plane and texture PTAA HTL layer. d) The reflectivity of real devices with plane and texture PTAA HTL layer compared with device by further adding anti-reflection (AR) coating.
Optical results show that the proportion of light captured by the active layer of device is increased in most wavelength ranges. Though the increased absorption at around $400~\mathrm{nm}$ due to PTAA cannot contribute to the final photocurrent, its negative effect on the short-circuit current is limited due to the small proportion of photons in sunlight spectrum. This deduction will be confirmed by the EQE measurements. On the contrary, the absorption of high-energy photons in PTAA reduces their impact on active perovskite layer, having positive effect on the stability of the perovskite solar cells.46-48
Device Performance. Perovskite solar cells have structure of ITO/PTAA/ MAPbI3(Cl)/PC61BM/BCP/Ag, as shown in Figure 4a, using plane or texture PTAA as HTL. The texture PTAA was obtained by method described in the previous section and used to let more light transmit into perovskite. A noticeable performance enhancement from the J-V measurements under illumination of $100\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ AM1.5G simulated light is shown in Figure 4b. The pure plane PTAA device, which is chosen as reference, achieves a considerable high PCE of $18.3\%$ with a $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.13\mathrm{V}$ , a $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $20.07\mathrm{\mA}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $80.7\%$ . As expected and notably, the champion PCE of device with texture HTL reaches $20.8\%$ with an impressive high $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.5\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a FF of $82.5\%$ and a $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.12\mathrm{V}$ . After adding the $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ anti-reflection film at the utmost outside of glass, the efficiency is further increased to $21.6\%$ , and the $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ is further increased to $23.26~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Figure 4c compares the measured EQEs and the corresponding integrated currents for these three devices. The integrated currents are 19.6, 21.4, and $22.2\ \mathrm{mA}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively for the reference, texture PTAA and ARtexture PTAA devices, verifying the J-V measurements. Obviously, the significant $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ improvement comes from the wide range of $420{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , consistent with the optical characterizations. The EQE drop at around $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ is just coincidence with the absorption of PTAA as mentioned earlier. To differentiate the contributions from light and other factor such as carrier extraction and collection, an apparent IQE is calculated by simply dividing the EQE by the light intensity subtracting the reflected portion. As shown in Figure 4d, though the apparent IQE of the texture PTAA device is still larger than that of plane PTAA device, their discrepancy is much smaller than that of EQE shown in Figure 4c. As shown in figure S7 in supporting information, the SEM images of the perovskite layers prepared on different substrates have the same uniform surface morphology, similar grain size, almost identical grain distribution and the same high film coverage.
At the same time, figure S8 in supporting information shows the XRD diffraction pattern of different substrates shown almost the same peak position and intensity. Because all other interfaces and formed layers are the same except for the PTAA/perovskite interface, the much smaller IQE discrepancy compared to the EQE discrepancy means that other factors except light management introduced by texture PTAA only contribute small photocurrent increase. That is, the better light management is the main source of $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ boost. Another positive influence of using texture PTAA as
HTL is the enhanced band edge absorption, which is also exemplified in the EQE curves in Figure 4c. As discussed above in Figure 1d, the scattering effect due to the rough textured PTAA can extend the optical path of light in perovskite layer, red shifting the EQE response about $6\mathrm{nm}$ . The rough estimation of this $6\mathrm{nm}$ red shift response results in about $0.3\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ current contribution. The introduction of texture PTAA does not weaken the advantages of inverted structure, such as better stability and less hysteresis. As can be seen in figure S9 in supporting information for the forward and reverse scan, the texture PTAA device shows negligible hysteresis. Similarly, the steady-state current and maximum power output are very stable, as shown in figure S10 in supporting information in 600 s duration. The applied voltage for this test is $0.98{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (optimum output point) and the AM 1.5G simulated light intensity is $100~\mathrm{\mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The reproducibility of the inverted PTAA texture perovskite solar cells is high, see figure S11 a, b, c and d in supporting information for the statistics of PCE from 17 batches of devices, including $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{OC}},\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF.
To verify the contributions from other than light management, the tests of transient photocurrent (TPC) and transient photovoltage (TPV) were conducted. The slightly faster TPC decay for the texture PTAA device (figure S12 in supporting information) indicates that though the textured interface can promote the charge collection but very limited. The substantially coincident TPV (figure S13 in supporting information) indicates that the defects and then the charge recombination are negligible changed by changing the HTL from flat to texture structure, consisting with the SEM shown in
Figure. S7 and XRD shown in Figure. S8 in supporting information. Further steadystate PL and time-resolved PL tests (shown in figure S14 a and b in supporting information) also show that the introduction of PTAA can quench PL efficiently but the difference between plane PTAA and texture PTAA is small.
As mentioned above, the texture interface was obtained by fabricating a series of devices with different textured PTAA as the substrate which were achieved by using different PTAA to PS ratios. The J-V curves are shown in figure 5a and EQEs are shown in figure 5b. As the ratio of PTAA to PS decreases, the $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ becomes larger first and then decreases, reaching the largest value at the ratio of 5:20. The EQE measurements confirm this variation of $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ coming from the increased photocurrent from almost the whole spectrum and thus indicates the possible better electric properties of the optimized texture PTAA. As shown in Table 1, the statistical results of 17 batches devices are clearly shown. To confirm or exclude this possible electric reason, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) technique was used to test the interface characteristics at DC bias of $0.9\mathrm{V}$ and under illumination simulated sunlight. As shown in figure S15 in supporting information, the series resistance and contact resistance are almost the same for different devices. Since the ITO and silver contact are the same for those devices, the almost same EIS results indicate that the change of the PTAA/perovskite interface morphology has a negligible effect on the extraction of holes, further confirming the conclusion made from TPV/TPV measurements. However, in figure S16 in supporting information, the difference in EQE stems from the fluctuated transmittances of different texture of glass/ITO/PTAA structures, because the fluctuation trend of the two types of curves is consistent.
Conclusion.
In conclusion, we have prepared the texture interface on the light-in side of the inverted device based on PTAA HTL to reduce internal light reflection, achieving a significant efficiency improvement from $18.3\%$ to $20.8\%$ . After the introduction of an anti-reflection film on the glass to let more light transmit into device, the efficiency was further improved, reaching the highest value of $21.6\%$ . Our experiments confirmed that optical management in the multi-layer perovskite solar cells is also of great significance in addition to the coordination of energy level alignment and the interface optimizations.
This work provides a viable case for improving the efficiency of inverted devices and lays the foundation for approaching S-Q limit. Although our efficiency of $21.6\%$ of inverted perovskite solar cell does not exceed that of the highest one of normal device, it is worthy to be noted that this high efficiency value is among the highest efficiencies of the inverted devices with low temperature process, see Table S1 in supporting information. Texture provides a viable solution for optical matching of perovskite solar cells, especially flexible devices which are not compatible with high temperature process.
## Experimental Section
Device Fabrication. First, the ITO ( $150~\mathrm{nm})$ substrate was cleaned by ultrasonic machine (KQ3200DV) in mixed detergent (Decon 90: deionized water $=25{:}1\$ ) at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then washed with deionized water for 4 times. The PS mixed PTAA(chlorobenzene) HTL was firstly deposited by spin coating consecutively at $400\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $3\mathrm{~s~}$ and 6000 rpm for 30 s followed by annealing at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a hotplate for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . The textured PTAA HTL layer was then achieved by dropping cyclohexane solvent onto the deposited mixed PTAA HTL layer to wash away PS in the film and then drying by spincoating. The third step was to deposit the perovskite layer on the textured PTAA HTL film, which was realized by spin coating perovskite precursor $(1.4~\mathrm{M})$ (as reported before) 16 at $400\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 3 s and $4000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s consecutively. The anti-solvent of chlorobenzene was dropped continuously after spin-coating for 12-15 s. The spincoated film was annealed at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 120 s and then $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30 minutes. After cooling down to room temperature, the deposition of the ETL was conducted by spin-coating $20\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{M}$ chlorobenzene solution at $5000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ and $0.5\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ BCP ethanol solution at $200\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s after 30 minutes in dry air 49. All spin-coating steps were carried out in nitrogen-filled glove box. Finally, $120\ \mathrm{nm}$ Ag was thermal evaporated to form the back contact. The final fabricated device area is $6\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ , defined by the overlap between ITO and Ag electrode.
Instruments and Characterization. The absorption and transmission spectra were recorded by using a Shimadzu UV-2550 Spectrophotometer and the reflection spectrum was measured with an integrating sphere. The J-V curves were obtained by a Keithley 2400 source meter adopting with a voltage step of $20~\mathrm{mV}$ and $0\mathrm{ms}$ delay time, under illumination of $100\mathrm{\mW}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (1.5 AM) simulated light from a Newport simulator (model 94043A). The EQE was obtained by calculating the photocurrent and light intensity measured using a lock-in amplifier (SR-830) by focusing a monochromatic beam of light onto devices and a calibrated silicon detector respectively. In the TPC/TPV measurement, the devices were illuminated by laser pulses ( $532~\mathrm{nm}$ , 6 ns width from an Nd:YAG laser) and the decay of signals were recorded by a 1-GHz Agilent digital oscilloscope (DSO-X3102A) with input impedance of $1\ M\Omega/50\ \Omega$ . PL and TRPL were obtained a Fluorolog-3 from HORIBA. Alternating-current (AC) impedance spectrum measurement, the electrochemical workstation was used (model 660D, Shanghai Chen Hua Instrument Co., Ltd., China). The SEM images were taken from a JSM-6700F scanning electron microscope. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy was measured with Thermo Fisher ESCALAB 250Xi. The X-ray diffraction patterns were obtained by an XRD-7000 X-ray diffractometer from SHIMADZU, Japan.
